sequenceDiagram
  %% Target design: orchestrator 2026-11-06, agent strip blocker (b). Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review: the queue is live application data, so it takes the path of database:query. The Gateway records the read, a Swamp step reads the Instance's registered Redis connection, and no agent runs. Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review: the report keeps what Horizon's Redis data holds without running the application; it drops jobs_per_minute, which needs Horizon's metrics snapshots.
  %% status: draft
  %% command: instance:queue
  %% kind: instance.queue
  autonumber
  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray
  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue
  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green
  %% lane R icon=database pill=target tone=amber
  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools
  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API
  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209
  participant R as Redis<br/>WireGuard
  CLI->>GW: instance:queue 12
  %%> Command:
  %%> - `orbit instance:queue 12`
  %%> - syntax: `<instance> [--state=pending|completed|failed] [--limit=N] [--json]`
  %%> Input:
  %%> - the Instance ID
  %%> Not included:
  %%> - `--state` (default pending) and `--limit` (1 to 50, default 50) only change which jobs the report lists
  Note over GW: Validate, record the read<br/>Instance 12 · task #5171
  %%> Requirements:
  %%> - your Node has an access grant to the Instance's Node
  %%> - the Instance owns a systemd Process whose command is `<php> artisan horizon`, and has a registered Redis connection with prefix REDIS, or the report is `available: false`
  %%> Where the credential lives:
  %%> - the Gateway stores the connection's password encrypted; at start it copies the password into the Swamp vault as `queue-read-intent-5171` (R3)
  %%> Result:
  %%> - task #5171 recorded with the full intent: the connection's host and port, the Horizon key prefix from the stored environment, and the state and limit; no lock, because a read changes nothing
  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-instance-queue
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - starts one Swamp run on serve (Node 209)
  %%> Inputs:
  %%> - the host, port and key prefix, the state and the limit
  %%> - the vault key name of the password, never the value
  GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task #5171
  %%> What you get:
  %%> - the task number, at once
  %%> Next:
  %%> - the CLI waits with progress, as for other tasks; a read is a short run, usually a few seconds
  SW->>R: Read Horizon's keys<br/>redis-cli on 209
  %%> How:
  %%> - the `redis-cli` client that the `swamp` role installs on 209; 209 reaches the host over WireGuard, with no SSH hop
  %%> - only read commands: ZCARD, ZREVRANGE, LLEN, SCARD and HMGET, sent in one pipeline; the password reaches the client on stdin
  %%> Reads:
  %%> - masters and their status, the supervisors' process counts
  %%> - the job counts of the three states, and the newest ids in the chosen state
  %%> - for each id, only the hash fields `name`, `queue`, `status`, `completed_at`, `failed_at` and `exception`; never `payload`
  %%> - each queue's length
  %%> Both:
  %%> - a 30 s limit; the output stops at 64 KiB
  alt Redis answers
    R-->>SW: values
    %%> Returns:
    %%> - the raw replies of the pipeline
    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event + replies
    %%> How the Gateway learns it:
    %%> - the replies come back as the run's terminal result; its shared run reader sees it; Swamp never calls the Gateway
    Note over GW: Shape the report<br/>task succeeded
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - casts and bounds every value again, builds the job links from the Instance's domain, and keeps only the fields of the report
    %%> - the first line of an exception, never its trace
    %%> Result:
    %%> - task #5171 succeeds with the report; no password and no job payload in it
  else Redis refuses or times out
    R--xSW: Error or timeout
    %%> When:
    %%> - a wrong password, no route to the host, or no answer within 30 s
    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>instance.queue_failed
    %%> The Gateway reads:
    %%> - a bounded error code and message, without Redis output
    Note over GW: Mark the task failed<br/>nothing changed · rerun is safe
    %%> Result:
    %%> - task #5171 fails; nothing changed in Redis
    %%> Retry:
    %%> - run the command again; a read is safe to repeat
  end
  loop every 2 s until terminal
    CLI->>GW: operation:show #5171
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - asks for the task's status every 2 seconds until it has ended
    GW-->>CLI: status + report
    %%> Returns:
    %%> - the current status, and the report once the task has ended
  end
